							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3380.msg38155#msg38155">Quote from: Blind Sage on October 04, 2007, 11:57:49 am</a></div><div class="quote"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3380.msg38123#msg38123">Quote from: Budaho on October 03, 2007, 07:41:38 pm</a></div><div class="quote"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3380.msg38099#msg38099">Quote from: Blind Sage on October 03, 2007, 12:27:00 pm</a></div><div class="quote">Bush definitely won&#039;t show, but I&#039;m talkin&#039; about him being treated with respect.  First, people really don&#039;t like him in Iran, lay person, scholar, or politician.  Second, it would a complete farce, the rulers in Iran would never allow for a open and free dialogue in a University with the president of this country.  I do want to ask this question though, I only saw a few bits of Ahmadinejad&#039;s session @ Columbia abut I&#039;m wondering how you think he was treated with disrespect, or were there other situations in which he was treated poorly while he was there?<br /></div><br /><a href="http&#58;//www.youtube.com/results?search_query=+Ahmadenijad+Columbia+Univ.+of+6&amp;search=Search" target="_blank">http://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=+Ahmadenijad+Columbia+Univ.+of+6&amp;search=Search</a><br /><br />decide for yourself.<br /><br />personally, i think being called a cruel and inhumane tyrant in the opening remarks of what is supposed to be an introduction for someone you invited to speak is disrespectful.  even if they are somewhat justified remarks.  i assume that this was the intention of the president of collumbia.  if he had wished to be respectful he could have posed questions to ahmadenijad about reports we have of things he&#039;s done we disagree with here.  but i don&#039;t see how calling someone cruel and inhumane to their face can be construed as anything but disrespectful.<br /><br />and personally, i think that the university memebers in iran who would be hosting president bush would be completely respectful.  but there would be massive demonstrations and protests.<br /></div><br />Yeah, I agree that calling a speaker names before he speaks, during the introduction to the event you invited him to, is somewhat disrespectful, though, I don&#039;t know that the university is beholden to treat him with a level of respect higher than they would hold anyone else. Nor are they beholden to use kid gloves with someone they strongly disagree with, and believe is dangerous, political leader or not.<br /><br /></div><br />oh, i agree with you.&nbsp; i don&#039;t think they were beholden to treat him with any great deal of respect, i heard an interview with the collubia president where he said in their letter of introduction that they would be a somewhat hostile host and audience(can&#039;t remember his terminology but...).&nbsp; but the point was that in iran being inhospitable rude and or disrespectful to any guest but most especially an invited guest is one of the biggest cultural taboos imaginable (hearsay i have little direct experience with iranians who haven&#039;t been americans for most of their lives but...).&nbsp; so for the collumbia president to act as he did, while not entirely inappropriate by american standards, was completely unacceptable by iranian standards.&nbsp; to the point where it even earned decries from ahmadinejad&#039;s staunchest opponents in iran, and rallied support behind him there like hasn&#039;t been seen in several years.&nbsp; making it at least a blunder.<br /><br /><br />and whats up with the edit on the initial post of the thread?&nbsp; </div>
						</td>
					</tr>
